 ### Octree-GS Rendering
-Octree-GS Rendering extends the standard pipeline by integrating an octree structure for efficient visibility culling and feature storage. It initializes a `GaussianModel` with dataset-specific parameters including `feat_dim`, `n_offsets`, `fork`, `use_feat_bank`, `appearance_dim`, `add_opacity_dist`, `add_cov_dist`, `add_color_dist`, `add_level`, `visible_threshold`, `dist2level`, `base_layer`, `progressive`, and `extend` [S2, unofficial]. A compatibility patch is applied to `gaussian_renderer.generate_neural_gaussians` to replace `ape_code[0]` with `ape_code` [S2, unofficial]. The pipeline sets an anchor mask for the current view using `gaussians.set_anchor_mask(view.camera_center, scene.loaded_iter, view.resolution_scale)` [S2, unofficial]. It then pre-filters voxels to determine visibility using `prefilter_voxel(view, gaussians, pipeline, background)` [S2, unofficial]. Finally, it renders the scene using `render(view, gaussians, pipeline, background, visible_mask=visible, ape_code=args.ape)` where `ape_code` is an integer argument with a default value of 10 [S2, unofficial]. The rendered image is clamped to $[0.0, 1.0]$ and saved, and the number of visible Gaussians per view is recorded in `per_view_count.json` [S2, unofficial].
+Octree-GS Rendering extends the standard pipeline by integrating an octree structure for efficient visibility culling and feature storage. It initializes a `GaussianModel` with dataset-specific parameters including `feat_dim`, `n_offsets`, `fork`, `use_feat_bank`, `appearance_dim`, `add_opacity_dist`, `add_cov_dist`, `add_color_dist`, `add_level`, `visible_threshold`, `dist2level`, `base_layer`, `progressive`, and `extend` [S3, unofficial]. A compatibility patch is applied to `gaussian_renderer.generate_neural_gaussians` to replace `ape_code[0]` with `ape_code` [S3, unofficial]. The pipeline sets an anchor mask for the current view using `gaussians.set_anchor_mask(view.camera_center, scene.loaded_iter, view.resolution_scale)` [S3, unofficial]. It then pre-filters voxels to determine visibility using `prefilter_voxel(view, gaussians, pipeline, background)` [S3, unofficial]. Finally, it renders the scene using `render(view, gaussians, pipeline, background, visible_mask=visible, ape_code=args.ape)` where `ape_code` is an integer argument with a default value of 10 [S3, unofficial]. The rendered image is clamped to $[0.0, 1.0]$ and saved, and the number of visible Gaussians per view is recorded in `per_view_count.json` [S3, unofficial].
 
